# Best Practices (Full-Stack)

Created: 2026-02-02 05:40 UTC  
Last reviewed: 2026-02-02 05:44 UTC

These are “default-good” practices for shipping changes quickly without losing reliability.

## Scope
- Small, reviewable diffs
- Clear verification
- Safe rollouts
- Durable documentation

## Change Hygiene
- Prefer small PRs with a single coherent intent.
- Avoid mixing refactors with behavior changes unless you must.
- Keep naming and structure consistent with the surrounding codebase.
- Remove debug logs, unused exports, and dead code before opening a PR.

## Testing & Verification
- Add at least one regression test per bugfix (when feasible).
- Prefer testing user-visible behavior (UI state, API responses) over implementation details.
- If tests are slow/flaky, isolate the smallest set that proves correctness and document the rest.

## Safety Defaults
- Default to read-only operations for external systems.
- Treat “writes” (DB, prod infra, deploys, schema changes) as explicitly gated actions.
- Prefer reversible rollouts (feature flags, canaries, staged deploys).

## Documentation
- If you change behavior or workflow, update docs in the same patch.
- Keep docs runnable: copy-pasteable commands and concrete examples.
- Prune or consolidate stale docs to avoid multiple “truths”.

## Debugging Loop (Fast)
1) Reproduce (tightest steps possible)
2) Observe (logs, DB, metrics) with a time window
3) Hypothesize (1–2 top candidates)
4) Verify (smallest experiment)
5) Fix + regression test
6) Capture evidence (commands, outputs, links) in the PR

## What “Believable” Looks Like in PRs
- A reviewer can answer:
  - “What changed?”
  - “Why is it correct?”
  - “How was it tested?”
  - “What’s the risk?”
  - “How do we rollback?”
